Bingo. A key concept that helps immensely is to synthesize determinism with these fundamentally non-deterministic tools. Two clear ways to do that are to break the overall work down into smaller pieces and to constrain the outputs of each piece. Clear context, such as subagents, and a forced return schema, like structured outputs, can get you pretty far on both of these things.

We have all these amazing building blocks, and the real value is now in figuring out the right ways to piece them together.

@Vtrivedy10: "So I think structured outputs used to be overrated before, but actually underrated and underused today as we’ve gone 'agent first, less workflowy.' Structured outputs previously defined a workflow contract/schema when passing data between nodes, but today we should use hooks/middleware to run deterministic code after certain actions. That’s easier to do reliably with structured outputs. Structured outputs with subagents is interesting; the subagent runs in isolated context to do a certain job, and we can be sure the main agent gets the keys and content we think it needs for the next step. A lot of agents are fuzzy workflow executors, so it helps to bring hooks/output contract tooling to these."

