Open models have crossed an intelligence threshold. We're helping users utilize them by integrating them into our products, building open harnesses around them, and measuring latency and cost gains without sacrificing performance for many tasks.

Many products that couldn't exist before are now possible with the economics of open model inference and fine-tuning. Come harness engineer an open model with us using deepagents and build a data moat with traces that you can use to hill climb or fine-tune.

@MiniMax_AI: "Open models aren't 'almost there' anymore. Independent evaluations from @langchain confirm: MiniMax M2.7 matches closed frontier models on core agent tasks at ~20× cheaper, 2–4× faster."

This is why we should all be deeply promiscuous across models. Every model has its own unique strengths, but all we care about is providing users with a great experience as quickly and affordably as possible.

Often, the best approach is to use a bespoke multi-model harness that optimizes performance, cost, and latency. For example, GLM5 or Minimax or Arcee can handle most work execution, while GPT-5.4 can serve as an oracle or judge when needed. Gemini Flash is useful for visual reasoning tasks, and Opus can enhance frontend taste. Don't be tied to a single model; utilize subagents and handoffs as necessary. Build a set of evaluations that reflect a good agent/user experience in production and switch models as soon as you can improve on an important axis. Your users and wallet will thank you.

@_Evan_Boyle: "Introducing RubberDuck in Copilot CLI: a new builtin subagent for cross model family escalations. Sonnet will now proactively solicit input from GPT 5.4 and vice versa. In our most difficult benchmark subsets, this results in a massive 5% improvement in resolution rates."

