Love seeing this open-sourced. Had a great chat with @nicoalbanese10 some weeks ago where he hinted at something like this. Open Agents provides a full stack, including UI, authentication, workflows, and sandbox capabilities. In contrast, #DeepAgent from @LangChain focuses on the agent runtime as a library, allowing full choice over infrastructure.

#DeepAgent offers flexibility with any LLM provider, pluggable sandbox backends, a composable middleware stack, async sub-agents for parallel work, human-in-the-loop configurations, persistent agent memory, and full tracing capabilities. All features are validated with over seven evaluation suites. 

@rauchg notes that this open-source platform is essential for building internal or user-facing coding agents, especially given the shift in software companies' focus from code to the means of production. If you're developing an agentic coding platform, this is a valuable resource.

Here’s a good application of harness permissions. Programmatically Enforced Auto-Research involves auto-research loops that usually expose a set of files the agent is allowed to edit to hill climb a metric or evaluations. The enforcement effectively prevents the model from modifying certain files, allowing for explicit enforcement rather than relying on prompts, which can lead to models justifying unauthorized edits.

Auto-research loops come with additional context, but you can deny write access to sensitive locations, which is crucial for blocking read access to important areas like verifier logic.

@sydneyrunkle: "New in deepagents: filesystem permissions. Shared resources and org-wide policies are exactly the kind of files you want your agent to read but never overwrite. Filesystem permissions let you enforce that with simple declarative allow/deny rules!"

